{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794005143,"nonce":"2JVr0lOuJjWANbmIPi2JOPKZ6qM=","message":"Design: typed number array part (int first, float after).\n\nA table's arr box is either the generic list of boxes or, new, a dense List\u003ci64\u003e under the Kind::Int list tag: every element a non-nil integer, len = count. Same ListHeader layout, so # and bounds checks are unchanged. The collector scans blocks conservatively, so a flat buffer needs nothing from it; weak-table walks in host_gc must skip int lists.\n\nEntry: a typed int store at index 1 into the shared empty array makes an int list; a typed append or overwrite into an int list stays flat. Anything else (nil, float, string, table, a hole, a metatable that may answer) converts to the generic list once, one way. zl_arr_own and arr_of convert, so every existing consumer (next, ipairs, sort, insert, remove, concat, unpack) is safe by construction; hot paths (rawgeti, rawseti, typed inline reads, typed stores) get a flat path.\n\nReads from a flat array under a typed Int element read raw; under any other static type they box (shared box up to 2047, else one allocation).\n\nTarget: pipeline (1.5M-element int arrays, 0.31 s vs 0.22 s LuaJIT), integer appends (about 590 instructions each today).","files":null}]}